# Visual identity

BioReserve OS is inspired by professional desktop operating systems from the mid-1990s.

The objective is not to recreate Windows 95 exactly, but to create a believable fictional operating system that could plausibly have existed during that period drawing inspiration from the Windows 95 user interface.

Preserve the retro visual identity even when modern design conventions would normally suggest alternative styling.

Avoid introducing modern SaaS aesthetics.

# Colour palette

Use a consistent colour palette throughout the application.

Preferred colours:

- Window background: dark graphite (`#3A3737`)
- Taskbar: dark graphite (`#3A3737`)
- Primary text: warm off-white (`#FAF6ED`)
- Accent colour: muted grey-green
- Title bars: deep saturated blue
- Borders: dark graphite

The desktop background uses a custom wallpaper rather than a solid colour.

Do not introduce bright modern accent colours unless explicitly requested.

# Windows

Desktop programs should appear and behave as movable operating-system windows.

Windows should:

- have crisp rectangular borders;
- use subtle bevelled edges to suggest depth;
- clearly distinguish title bars from content;
- be greyed out when inactive;
- display a close ('X') button in the top-right corner of the title bar;
- remain visually consistent throughout the application.

Different applications may have different default sizes where appropriate.

# Controls

Buttons, inputs and controls should reflect the visual language of mid-1990s desktop software.

Prefer:

- rectangular controls;
- subtle bevel effects;
- restrained colour usage;
- compact spacing.

Avoid:

- rounded corners;
- glass effects;
- transparency;
- floating panels;
- oversized controls;
- excessive whitespace.

# Icons

Icons should:

- follow a pixel-art inspired design language;
- remain simple and immediately recognisable;
- use a restrained colour palette;
- visually resemble classic desktop application icons.

# Overall atmosphere

The interface should feel professional, practical and authentic.

It should resemble software designed for everyday operational use rather than a nostalgic parody of 1990s computing.

When visual authenticity and modern UI conventions conflict, prefer authenticity unless usability would be significantly reduced.